; Stage 15 W9 defect 11, site 3 of 4: `(dyn %s): '%s' is not a declared protocol`
; — two `%s` fed by a ONE-substitution helper, so the pre-fix compiler
; SEGFAULTED with no output when it fired. `fmt-2s` is the correct helper and
; this fixture is what keeps it correct: a corrected format string that nothing
; executes is one edit away from regressing.
;
; RE-POINTED by Stage 15 W9 defect 21 (2026-08-03), per the instruction the
; previous version of this comment left behind. It used to reach the message by
; exploiting a bug: `conformance-*` canonicalized their keys with
; `strip-ns-qualifier` (Stage 12 N4 decision 9) while `protocol-lookup` matched
; the raw spelling, so `(extend Cat dp/Describe)` recorded the conformance under
; the bare `Describe`, `admit-erased-conformance` found it and admitted the box,
; and `dyn-vtable-method-irname` then looked the protocol up under the QUALIFIED
; spelling and found nothing. Defect 21 fixed that — a protocol is a namespaced
; entity, `dp/Describe` resolves from any namespace, and extend/box/dispatch
; works end to end (examples/w9-dyn-ns.nuc pins the working path).
;
; What still legitimately reaches the message is a `dyn` position naming a
; protocol that was never declared, which `emit-box-value` now asks about BEFORE
; conformance (via the shared `dyn-require-protocol`) — otherwise an undeclared
; protocol was reported as the misleading "type 'Cat' does not conform to the
; protocol". The `extend` below is deliberately KEPT and now SUCCEEDS, so this
; file still exercises the cross-namespace conformance path and proves the
; diagnostic fires on the genuine case rather than on the old inconsistency.
; `dp/Missing` is qualified so both `%s` substitutions still carry a non-trivial
; string — a regression to `fmt-s` would still segfault here.
(import-use "stdio.h")
(import-use allocator)
(import-prefixed w9-dynprotolib dp)

(defstruct Cat n:i32)
(extend Cat dp/Describe)
(defn describe ((self (ref Cat))):i32 (return (+ 100 (self n))))

(defn boxed ():(dyn dp/Missing)
  (return (Cat 5)))

(defn main ():i32 (return 0))
